From e1574f2752918aac3a2269b048bd856f1e599387 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 26 Jun 2026 17:50:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2012/2064] windowing/gbm: pipeline the GUI present - gate GPU
 render and CPU pacing on different flips

The async present path (CDRMAtomic NONBLOCK commit + explicit fencing)
inserted, before each eglSwapBuffers, a GPU-side wait on the *previous* flip's
KMS out-fence, and after the swap a CPU wait (eglClientWaitSyncKHR,
EGL_FOREVER) on that same fence:

    fd = TakeOutFenceFd();        // out-fence of the immediately previous commit
    CreateKMSFence(fd);
    WaitSyncGPU();                // GPU defers this frame's work until that flip
    ...swap...
    SetInFenceFd(gpuFence);       // KMS defers scanout until this render completes
    WaitSyncCPU();                // CPU blocks until that same flip

Both waits targeting the immediately-preceding flip make the render pipeline
exactly one frame deep: the GPU cannot start frame N+1 until frame N is on
screen, and the present thread is likewise pinned to that flip. For a trivial
scene that is fine, but a non-trivial GUI composite (a scrolling plot, or the
FBO/HDR composite path) intermittently needs more than the post-vblank slice
of a refresh interval, loses the next vblank, and the flip slips by one -
giving an alternating one-/two-vblank cadence that averages ~40fps on a 60Hz
panel. CPU and GPU both sit well below capacity because the time is spent
idle-waiting on the fence, not computing.

Split the two waits so the pipeline is two frames deep with one frame of slack:

  - GPU render gate -> the flip *before* last (N-2). N-2's out-fence guards the
    buffer mesa is about to recycle as this frame's render target, so it is the
    correct and sufficient gate; it lets the GPU render this frame while the
    previous flip is still pending instead of stalling on it. Implemented by
    holding (a dup of) the previous out-fence one frame in m_kmsFenceFd and
    issuing the GPU wait on it via the new WaitSyncGPU(int fd).

  - CPU pacing -> the previous flip (N-1). Blocking the present thread until the
    previous flip lands is the back-pressure that paces the GUI render loop to
    the display refresh. The GUI loop (CApplication::Run) has no other frame
    limiter, so without this it free-runs above refresh on light scenes -
    e.g. ~79fps at 1080p on a 60Hz panel - burning GPU on frames that are never
    scanned out. Because the wait overlaps the render just submitted by
    eglSwapBuffers, capping at refresh does not reintroduce the stall.

Net effect: GPU-bound scenes (e.g. 1440p) run at whatever the GPU sustains
instead of stalling to 40, and GPU-light scenes are capped cleanly at the
refresh rate instead of wasting work above it. Cost is at most one extra frame
of presentation latency, only to the extent the extra pipeline slot is filled.

Correctness is unchanged:
  - The freshly rendered front buffer is still the one committed to KMS.
  - KMS still waits for this frame's GPU render via IN_FENCE_FD, so no
    partially-rendered frame is scanned out.
  - The buffer mesa recycles is older than the N-2 fence the GPU gates on, and
    the GBMUtils buffer queue keeps the on-screen and pending buffers locked,
    so a buffer still being scanned out is never handed back for rendering.

Also fold the byte-identical fence sequence that the GL and GLES PresentRender()
implementations each carried into FencePreSwap()/FencePostSwap() on the shared
base CWinSystemGbmEGLContext, and clear m_kmsFence after destroy in
WaitSyncCPU() (a frame whose previous commit produced no out-fence skips
CreateKMSFence, so the stale handle could otherwise be double-destroyed).

(cherry picked from commit 8bd17d59c1dc35cf1937f3ab79378d64cc8fe4b1)
